WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:06.859
In my last video, I posed the following
question:

00:00:06.859 --> 00:00:11.200
If you take n points on a circle, then
connect every pair of them with a line,

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.670
how many sections to these lines cut
the circle into?

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:18.800
What was strange is that when n is less than 6, and when n is 10 for some reason,

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.700
the answer is always a power of 2, but for
all other values of n,

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:27.220
the answer seems completely unrelated
powers of 2.  What love about this problem

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:30.590
is that it brings together many
disparate concept: counting functions,

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:34.610
graphs,  one of Euler's famous
equations, and Pascal's triangle.

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:39.720
You might be wondering if changing the
placement of points change the number of

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:40.399
sections

00:00:40.399 --> 00:00:44.510
It actually can! For instance, watch how
this small region in the middle

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:48.440
disappears if we adjust things so that
three lines go through the same point.

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:52.440
But if we add the restriction that no
three lines can go to the same point,

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:56.080
the number sections depends only on the
number of points not their placement,

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:00.060
as you're about to see.   I think it's fair
to call this a hard problem

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:04.040
and in solving hard problems, it's a
good idea to ask simpler questions about

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:04.890
the same setup.

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:09.500
In this case I have two questions for
you: 1) How many lines are there?

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:14.040
and 2) At how many points to these lines
intersect within the circle?

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:18.690
For the first question, every line
corresponds uniquely with the pair of

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:19.220
points

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:22.730
and likewise every pair of points gives us
a unique line.

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:25.960
Luckily, counting the number of pairs in
a set

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.250
is common enough in math that we have
specific notation for it:

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:32.380
"n choose 2" which we evaluate as n

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:36.500
times (n -1) divided by 2.   To see where
this formula comes from,

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:39.800
notice that you have n options for the
first element of the pair,

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:43.390
which we multiply by the (n -1)
remaining options for the second element.

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:48.390
But this would double count each pair so
we divide by two.

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:52.980
For instance, when n equals 7,  7 choose 2 is 7 times 6 over 2,

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:56.380
or 21, so there are 21 pairs of points,
and hence

00:01:56.380 --> 00:02:00.170
21 lines.  With, say, a hundred points

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:03.560
counting lines directly would be a
nightmare, but we can compute it

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:07.230
as 100 choose 2, which is 100 times 99 divided by 2,

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:11.320
or 4,950.  The number intersection
points

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:14.840
is a bit more subtle.  While every
intersection point corresponds with a

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:15.250
unique

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:19.100
pair of lines, there are many pairs of lines that don't intersect within the circle,

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:22.680
so we can't just count the pairs of lines.  What we can do,

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:26.970
though is associate each intersection
point with a set of four point on the

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:27.480
circle

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.760
since this association goes the other
way around, in that

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.840
every quadruplet of points gives a
unique intersection point.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:38.600
Just look at that, isn't that elegant?  This means the number intersection points

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:41.890
is the same as the number quadruplets
of our starting points.

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:45.820
The function "n choose 4" counts
quadruplets in a set,

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:49.610
and we evaluate it by taking n times (n-1) times (n-2) times

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:50.410
(n-3)

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:54.310
all divided by 1 times 2 times
3 times 4.  The derivation of this

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:56.690
formula is similar to that of n choose 2,

00:02:56.690 --> 00:03:00.700
You multiply in the number of options you have for each successive entry,

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:07.260
Then divide by the extent to which you've over counted.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:09.340
For instance, with n=4,

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:13.000
4 choose 4 is 1, and indeed there's
just one intersection point.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:17.560
6 choose 4 is 15, so when n=6 there are 15
intersection points.

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:21.990
And if n were 100, even though the
prospect of counting intersection point

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:23.060
is horrifying

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:28.100
we can nevertheless deduce that there
will be 3,921,225

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:32.140
of them.  Now, how does this help us count sections in the circle,

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:35.490
you might ask.  Well it will once we
consider graphs

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:39.800
an Euler's formula.  No, no, not function
graphs, and not that e to the pi i stuff.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:42.820
The word graph can also refer to a
set of points,

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:47.020
referred to as "vertices", along with a
set of lines connecting some of these points

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:52.300
which we call "edges".  Notice if we count
the number of vertices in this graph

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:55.640
then subtract the numbers edges, then add
the number

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:59.270
region this graph cuts space into,
along with that outer region,

00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:01.350
we get 2.

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:04.930
If we do the same thing with this other
graph...well...we get

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:10.349
2 again.

00:04:10.349 --> 00:04:14.090
This isn't a coincidence, you could do
this with any graph and as long as your

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:16.049
edges don't intersect each other

00:04:16.049 --> 00:04:19.479
the answer is always 2.  If edges could
intersect,

00:04:19.479 --> 00:04:22.139
then you could just change the number
regions without changing the number of

00:04:22.139 --> 00:04:23.229
vertices and edges,

00:04:23.229 --> 00:04:26.990
so course it would be nonsense. This
relation is known as

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:30.610
"Euler's Characteristic Formula", and it's
easy to see where the name comes from

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:33.990
since "Euler's" is German for beautiful.  If
you're curious,

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:37.310
the reason we write "F" for the number
regions is because the formula

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:40.830
traditionally refers to the number of
vertices, edges and faces

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:44.840
of 3d polyhedra. In another video,
I'll explain why this is true

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:48.820
but here let's just use it to solve our
circle problem. Our set up is already a

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:49.290
graph

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:53.720
with n vertices and n choose 2
edges, one between each pair of points.

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:57.000
But we cannot apply Euler's
characteristic formula directly, since

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:00.900
the edges intersect many times;  n choose
4 times to be exact.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:03.940
However, if we consider each intersection
point

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:07.810
to be a vertex, meaning our original
lines must be chopped up at these points,

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:11.860
and if we also include the segment of
the circle connecting these points

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:15.880
as new edges, we have a graph perfectly
suited for Euler's formula.

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:19.280
In particular the number regions in this
picture

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:23.370
is the numbers edges in the new graph
minus the number vertices

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:25.280
plus 2

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:28.130
Since our new graph retains the n
original vertices,

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:32.110
and ads on another n choose 4 for
intersection points, we replace the minus

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:32.780
V term

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:36.970
with minus n minus n choose 4.  To
find the number

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:40.420
edges, note that the intersection points can
be seen as

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:44.410
adding two edges each, since each one takes 2
existing lines

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:47.610
and then cuts them into four smaller
pieces.

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:50.660
For example, three lines intersecting at
2 points

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:54.550
would be cut into 3+2*2
equals 7 smaller pieces.

00:05:54.550 --> 00:05:59.500
Four lines intersecting at three points
would be cut into 4+2*3

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:02.910
equals 10 smaller pieces.  And in our
circle diagram

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:06.590
n choose 2 lines intersecting at
n choose four points

00:06:06.590 --> 00:06:10.960
are cut into  n choose 2 plus 2 times
n choose 4 smaller pieces.

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:13.970
plus another n for the circle
segments we're now considering to be

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:14.560
edges.

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:17.560
Going back to our formula, we can replace the "E"

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:21.360
with n choose 2, plus 2 times n choose 4, plus n.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:24.710
Combining like terms we see that our
graph cuts the 2d plane

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:28.210
into 2 plus n choose 2 plus n choose 4 smaller pieces.

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:31.820
Since we're concerned with counting the
regions inside the circle

00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:35.320
we can disregard that outer region and
write our answer as 1

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:38.600
plus n choose 2 plus n choose 4.
Great!

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:42.860
We found the answer!  But why on earth does
this formula relate to powers of 2

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:46.290
for n less than 6, then again when
n is 10?

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:49.410
It's not just a coincidence, it has to do
with Pascal triangle.

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:53.360
Pascal's triangle is constructed like
this: each term

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:56.990
is the sum of the two terms above it.  If
you add up each row,

00:06:56.990 --> 00:07:01.309
you get a successive power of 2.

00:07:01.309 --> 00:07:03.719
To convince yourself of this, notice 
that each term is

00:07:03.719 --> 00:07:07.989
added into the following row twice, so
the sum of each row should be twice the

00:07:07.989 --> 00:07:09.059
sum of the row before it.

00:07:09.059 --> 00:07:12.979
The function n choose k is closely related
to this triangle,

00:07:12.979 --> 00:07:16.709
in that the the kth entry of the nth row, where
counting start at 0,

00:07:16.709 --> 00:07:21.759
is always n choose k.  For instance,
to find 5 chose 3 in the triangle,

00:07:21.759 --> 00:07:25.079
count down to the 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5th row,

00:07:25.079 --> 00:07:28.369
then go in 0, 1, 2, 3.  And indeed,

00:07:28.369 --> 00:07:32.969
5 choose 3 is 10. This means that the answer to
our circle problem

00:07:32.969 --> 00:07:36.169
for n is the sum of the  0th, 2nd,

00:07:36.169 --> 00:07:40.050
and 4th entires in the nth row of
Pascal's triangle.  For instance,

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:43.610
If n equals 5, we can see that we just
have to add 1

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:47.189
10 and 5.  Since each term is the sum
of the two above it,

00:07:47.189 --> 00:07:51.500
this is the same as adding the entire
fourth row which we know is a power of 2.

00:07:51.539 --> 00:07:55.259
Likewise for smaller values of n,
the answer is going to be the sum of the

00:07:55.259 --> 00:07:56.469
(n-1)st row.

00:07:56.469 --> 00:07:59.989
And hence a power of 2.  However,

00:07:59.989 --> 00:08:03.429
when n = 6, and we will relate the
terms to the fifth row,

00:08:03.429 --> 00:08:07.979
notice that we're not adding the entire
row since we missed that last term

00:08:07.979 --> 00:08:11.419
so we only get 31. When n equals 10,

00:08:11.419 --> 00:08:16.169
we're summing  precisely half of the ninth
row, so the answer is half of 2 to the 9th,

00:08:16.169 --> 00:08:20.439
which is 2 to the 8th.  So, to recap,

00:08:20.439 --> 00:08:24.449
Turn our diagram into a graph suitable for
Euler's characteristic formula

00:08:24.449 --> 00:08:27.819
by adding all the intersection points
as vertices and cutting up all the

00:08:27.819 --> 00:08:28.329
edges

00:08:28.329 --> 00:08:31.789
Next, count the number of lines and
intersection points

00:08:31.789 --> 00:08:35.169
by relating them to pairs and quadruplets
of our starting points.

00:08:35.169 --> 00:08:39.620
And, finally use Euler's formula to deduce
the number of sections,

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:44.079
then relate this to powers of 2 using Pascal's triangle.

